  "Message": "feat: direct OpenAI transport for managed remote model requests\n\nThe terminal-server's litt:model:request channel previously had only\nan OpenRouter transport. Every managed model request — even when the\nCLI routed to an OpenAI model — went through openrouter.ai, producing\nroutes like \"openrouter/openai/gpt-oss-20b:nitro\" and reporting\nprovider: \"openrouter\" for what the UI labeled as OpenAI.\n\nRoot cause: streamModelForRemoteClient was hardcoded to call\nopenrouter.ai with OPENROUTER_API_KEY. No direct OpenAI transport\nexisted. RemoteModelProvider.providerId was hardcoded to \"openrouter\".\nThe CLI sent only the OpenRouter slug, never the provider-native id.\n\nFix (4 parts, all in this commit):\n\n1. terminal-server/litt-code.ts: Add streamOpenAIDirect() that calls\n   api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions with the provider-native model\n   id. Refactor the OpenRouter path into streamOpenRouterRemote().\n   Both share consumeSSEStream() for identical SSE parsing. The meta\n   event reports the ACTUAL provider (\"openai\" or \"openrouter\"),\n   never a hardcoded value.\n\n   streamModelForRemoteClient now does server-side transport selection:\n   - If providerHint=\"openai\" + real OPENAI_API_KEY (sk-proj-*) +\n     model id has no \"/\" (not an OpenRouter slug) -\u003e direct OpenAI\n   - Otherwise -\u003e OpenRouter fallback with the OpenRouter slug\n   - Guards against an OpenRouter key (sk-or-v1-*) misplaced in\n     OPENAI_API_KEY — detects the prefix and falls back to OpenRouter\n     instead of sending an OpenRouter key to api.openai.com\n\n2. terminal-server/model-relay.ts: Pass providerHint and\n   openRouterModelId from the Socket.IO payload through to the\n   transport selector. Billing now records usage for both \"openai\"\n   and \"openrouter\" providers (was openrouter-only).\n\n3. packages/litt-cli/src/lib/runtime-client.ts: streamModel() accepts\n   providerHint and openRouterModelId options, sends them in the\n   litt:model:request payload.\n\n4. packages/litt-cli/src/lib/remote-model-provider.ts:\n   RemoteModelProvider.providerId is now mutable — initialized to the\n   CLI's routing hint, updated to the server's truthful meta event\n   report. The meta event and ModelResult use the server-reported\n   provider, not a hardcoded \"openrouter\". Constructor accepts\n   providerHint and openRouterModelId.\n\n5. packages/litt-cli/src/ink/controller.ts: resolveModelProvider()\n   sends routed.providerModelId (native id) as the primary model,\n   routed.servedBy as the providerHint, and routed.openRouterModelId\n   as the fallback slug. Previously it sent only the OpenRouter slug.\n\nRegression tests (9 tests, all passing):\n- OpenAI selection -\u003e calls api.openai.com, not openrouter.ai\n- OpenRouter selection -\u003e calls openrouter.ai, not api.openai.com\n- OpenAI hint but no OPENAI_API_KEY -\u003e falls back to OpenRouter,\n  reported as \"openrouter\" (not \"openai\")\n- OpenRouter fallback cannot claim direct OpenAI in metadata\n- OpenRouter key in OPENAI_API_KEY slot -\u003e falls back, not treated\n  as direct OpenAI\n- Direct OpenAI route uses provider-native model id, never :nitro\n- No provider keys at all -\u003e throws clear error\n- OpenRouter key in OPENAI slot + no OPENROUTER_API_KEY -\u003e throws\n- OpenAI hint with OpenRouter slug model (has /) -\u003e falls back\n\nThis fixes the \"openrouter/openai/gpt-oss-20b:nitro\" route that\nappeared when a signed-in user selected an OpenAI model. The same\nfix applies to Termux/Android — the CLI sends the provider hint and\nboth model IDs; the terminal-server selects the transport.\n\nGenerated with [Devin](https://devin.ai)\n\nCo-Authored-By: Devin \u003c158243242+devin-ai-integration[bot]@users.noreply.github.com\u003e",
  "Tags": [],
  "Fingerprint": "f00cc22118c67a53391864be1dc8f94013e818ad:terminal-server/__tests__/provider-transport-selection.test.ts:generic-api-key:263"
 },
